given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46f974ec-ee8c-52ea-81e3-8930b26a84ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272995} {:answer/id #uuid "56b6f0bb-39ce-56d1-9d4c-0e806a85f6f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273127} {:answer/id #uuid "5cec238a-374b-5759-b68b-9e4d089dfed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273186} {:answer/id #uuid "a845ee63-b246-59cb-9bad-0e56415df27f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273843} {:answer/id #uuid "dd517fc2-8ad6-55dd-ba11-0b285c3859fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274334}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cec238a-374b-5759-b68b-9e4d089dfed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273186} :answer-field/id #uuid "66f8af18-559d-5cfa-a4d4-da12c0ae0ecd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0609c9a8-63cb-5946-854d-790ce4ad14b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-158100" :question/problem "![](images/00/003b4f77a413444989bcec8b9682465419a58ded55ed95cefb4f43c122bdc3d3.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14ca2bb0-45fa-515e-86a8-cce64866690b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272574} {:answer/id #uuid "604d7a59-1fef-523e-bf07-a9f5dd400e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273219} {:answer/id #uuid "6afe9e0d-cdbd-5294-ac31-28a3a86f03a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273310} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a6743f-b156-512b-8812-e7ac6baca636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273808} {:answer/id #uuid "dfde1b99-be1a-5fba-9ccf-784956f7918d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfde1b99-be1a-5fba-9ccf-784956f7918d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274365} :answer-field/id #uuid "73ce813a-1598-5c11-b720-fcf588d7f65b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ce01e6e-4d84-5a03-940c-8de7146f3c76" :question/math-academy-id "q-158101" :question/problem "![](images/27/2739a0225640ff20cc9678edf8c41ba45ff637b17a40d0322ca792bef1346f06.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192782 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19361250-d29e-5d0b-a043-83203d0cee3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272606} {:answer/id #uuid "33f098e1-00df-50e5-8501-8f96815fb855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272848} {:answer/id #uuid "5b214f56-9d2b-5034-83ab-15abf9fb5fd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273168} {:answer/id #uuid "92f42aa1-958f-59e5-a2f9-82732b5b8eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273664} {:answer/id #uuid "cb91942a-49ed-514c-827b-994c85e6200d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19361250-d29e-5d0b-a043-83203d0cee3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272606} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e2fa1ff-86ae-58d8-8acb-35ad8555761e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ec726d7a-9c79-5a1b-91e2-01dc3e4c2650" :question/math-academy-id "q-158102" :question/problem "![](images/42/42b23af4df7892df46461b01fd549ca56723dde0c08d3e88b71ea119bf45ba23.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a50740e-24d8-5780-9497-19c6ece3d12f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272613} {:answer/id #uuid "c5bd4857-ac42-501a-afee-68ed1f090a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274103} {:answer/id #uuid "d470a61d-9b60-594f-8de0-64bd4e327f55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274250} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ed8770-d0c9-5fd8-874c-51bd1000938a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274290} {:answer/id #uuid "e46caf60-f48c-5c19-a408-c2d5469c2e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e46caf60-f48c-5c19-a408-c2d5469c2e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274409} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b48ef03-f856-526f-b739-6f66d9042d61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f120679-716b-5edd-8658-50352ee4a1a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-158414" :question/problem "![](images/49/495b8aaac849e90ffd46f34f35dd4d3783c2c4fa35c3887c339387d31215d7af.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{4},\\, & 0<x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x+1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+2]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+0]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ba54c3f-064a-505e-a601-3acf3faeb1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272623} {:answer/id #uuid "2687fa37-7cd9-57df-ae31-bf65e49d2f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272723} {:answer/id #uuid "29886336-d545-55a2-be6c-36709fde9ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272756} {:answer/id #uuid "91f1887b-7e63-57c3-941d-9dd481993654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273659} {:answer/id #uuid "c66381cb-1bde-5b2c-a1e5-601c5da32b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29886336-d545-55a2-be6c-36709fde9ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272756} :answer-field/id #uuid "1939075e-3ac3-5a0b-88b8-649ddaf11e22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ced5dff-e7df-54bc-a45c-2aa3418fd883" :question/math-academy-id "q-158419" :question/problem "![](images/4a/4acac98ef7276cb1e90557ca161666a33ccf0a7627b9ebfcbbe44508b9e5f5bb.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{0}^{3}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "109d13ff-3bfe-5465-8e92-ac9059d764ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275624} {:answer/id #uuid "29e60057-5cb4-5015-95b0-91bee1f5e649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275856} {:answer/id #uuid "392e6a3e-2941-5807-afa5-d592600d687d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275984} {:answer/id #uuid "b23aee92-8125-55d2-a4af-7c2cd02f7163" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277072} {:answer/id #uuid "e071ea78-d1da-5cc3-8380-acd11f8d0e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "109d13ff-3bfe-5465-8e92-ac9059d764ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275624} :answer-field/id #uuid "b498bc2c-917d-5d5a-9ad4-b1ae4a48ec03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bac4e6a1-90fc-5ecd-80e4-898747c5b764" :question/math-academy-id "q-158424" :question/problem "![](images/81/819fa17f44f669746ba0c82d2d986f1cd5955a1d7d8144eda6922c0cb7813c9c.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3},\\, & 1\\le x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{4}{15}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{15}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{15}]-[\\frac{{1}^{4}}{15}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25fedb2c-bf2b-5eef-8ec0-bc146ad8d61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275821} {:answer/id #uuid "3848fe11-38a7-5fe9-90cc-d571fae7f82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275975} {:answer/id #uuid "3d6c5a79-0bcb-5037-be35-12c21b765dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276010} {:answer/id #uuid "ab4a3342-d68c-5d6f-bc28-9208d47fe5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277017} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a9a370-c4e2-5c31-9286-b1ba31d3cba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab4a3342-d68c-5d6f-bc28-9208d47fe5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277017} :answer-field/id #uuid "285132f1-bde6-5b4d-8614-dba9dd3d798c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f670c211-dff8-5382-9b62-e560f1a2abfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-158425" :question/problem "![](images/87/87c5e1b3307b2a42fff6a97408c0475dbe242bef4a9dbfbabcec407b3fa23ce1.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3},\\, & 0\\le x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(1)^{4}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278180 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fe4c108-0c0a-5e29-911c-d1646ab97905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275771} {:answer/id #uuid "40c140df-3b9c-5298-903d-5b271568acb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276042} {:answer/id #uuid "535322b9-be17-54df-a7a2-0c2483ef3419" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276217} {:answer/id #uuid "ade0f0bb-07c8-5c24-b4d4-641372fd68b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277038} {:answer/id #uuid "ca0393b2-a850-5c48-8662-d0108f8af66c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40c140df-3b9c-5298-903d-5b271568acb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276042} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3c08baf-5e84-59ed-8178-0fc243f082fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ba85730-5b8d-5d4c-8799-b386e641d64f" :question/math-academy-id "q-158427" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae6d25fae4127a74b38bcb9c0de8f44705dca81353c89557047e5d35a3465c5b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "427c9c68-1ef6-5faf-85f0-b60b26c9e8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276061} {:answer/id #uuid "67cf8a35-513b-5da7-851e-d389efdea9a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276407} {:answer/id #uuid "6fc1d034-4ce7-53ad-86ed-b2170e1c8f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276486} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2f50e6-13cd-5008-8539-204ab7524eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277281} {:answer/id #uuid "e4dc87e7-b7df-5745-bf8c-e4290eee23fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb2f50e6-13cd-5008-8539-204ab7524eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277281} :answer-field/id #uuid "81eec413-638f-5e83-9478-74f78dad803b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50d76297-0f50-5089-b83a-de45e6a36bc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-158428" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e540dc9309f2a5d1753760b4050c049e380705914d7289b47319de0b0696a9a.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{3}-1),\\, & \\frac{1}{2}<x\\le \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. $f(x)$ is negative for some value of $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}4({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1/2}^{3/2}({x}^{3}-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-x]}_{1/2}^{3/2} \\\\  & =4([\\frac{81}{64}-\\frac{3}{2}]-[\\frac{1}{64}-\\frac{1}{2}]) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{80}{64}-1) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{4}-1) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement I is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f6fe045-70ea-5894-a90b-388f3bedbcf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276027} {:answer/id #uuid "420f62d1-9078-5c1f-8386-e2b504eb643e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276054} {:answer/id #uuid "62138cea-0ce0-5fe0-a43e-c434297442e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276362} {:answer/id #uuid "ba70cada-15bf-5af8-9de3-0cf37e32754a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277132} {:answer/id #uuid "d2944c44-2492-51ff-b8ac-439157fc2325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "420f62d1-9078-5c1f-8386-e2b504eb643e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276054} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e5737c-8be4-59c6-b944-061e18639622" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0715ac14-20ca-5be9-8d09-d1a1ccc33941" :question/math-academy-id "q-158429" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c30c392223857c77cfc8619f1fa0d4b7fca20af618bdd424570cc3ed712f38b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-1,\\, & 1<x\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}-x]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[9-3]-[1-1] \\\\  & =6-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "892a8ae7-7816-5ec8-abd7-0f193b914c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276716} {:answer/id #uuid "994734ca-d6aa-5641-8d0e-63d406e741fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276853} {:answer/id #uuid "cef0cd2a-9051-554c-a6f5-91d00529319a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277308} {:answer/id #uuid "e65fda98-7388-56e8-99ca-9bfe1594e4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277488} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc62238-282a-5ff2-b84f-201b8a5eed6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "994734ca-d6aa-5641-8d0e-63d406e741fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276853} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd602e17-33cb-5cf6-97db-92756d77aaff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed33ac2f-6299-5342-b520-1399b2a77786" :question/math-academy-id "q-158684" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5dccdb1f8957bb15d7b2215320c6e632819a647bf4dc350ff850876bae6298ec.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-3,2]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/d5/d595e29d31743694c404b99c79c60e40b152d30083e633d4269ad4444be3f826.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/23/2336ff8a65dd397e8324384b9f9536f3c7d4ad2d76a81caeba28d7fe3ec229ef.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/26/26903667d45cecf81539025c4cda333129cf89600821b383f20d574c75e537b0.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0243a0f1-2e03-5947-a203-a74798f52121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275498} {:answer/id #uuid "1d167fdd-7c58-55ea-9621-e7c510872897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275743} {:answer/id #uuid "227d62f9-8ba3-5bb9-80bd-c1da5585045a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275786} {:answer/id #uuid "f68d95d4-11a1-5b68-a4f8-df5901c443ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277634} {:answer/id #uuid "fed55126-d8c5-5c16-a6ce-4064c6e9eb5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f68d95d4-11a1-5b68-a4f8-df5901c443ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277634} :answer-field/id #uuid "487c91f9-e32d-5423-97c7-0f4657cf1eca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1861489-bfb5-5c9b-ba84-97990e733cb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-158685" :question/problem "![](images/73/73f61de1a716545449439ed8875b465dc5aec6f3148e520ad028a96725f2a4f0.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[0,1]\\cup {2}$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained in the set $M.$\n\n![](images/84/84afb5331ef9244120d9f3d9b14e25fa2d19da31f8eb685c5d74b089e0d99289.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/c0/c0b35fafc6e628c36e41fa8df42e3a477d8aac4af0b4c3819fa0063e5bd014df.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ Notice that $C$ is a point of $M$ and $C$ belongs to any open neighborhood of $C.$\n\n![](images/e5/e54fa85543a79c18e00d3b03281d6774c98fdb03e9b5440b8f179ce5a0d09519.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3585e5cf-97a2-5a9f-9483-1363128dc2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275955} {:answer/id #uuid "72c53d80-bb03-50ef-96b9-e5708290dc29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276517} {:answer/id #uuid "8ffb6950-d856-55a4-a552-464de9c420fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276779} {:answer/id #uuid "bd749509-056c-5495-87c6-08d0205808fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277167} {:answer/id #uuid "d0bad058-e92c-5e6a-94cb-829624887a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0bad058-e92c-5e6a-94cb-829624887a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277325} :answer-field/id #uuid "a49af98b-856c-574a-b392-0ae041c650d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94c1ba54-750c-5dd8-b08c-c465652d3c9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-159264" :question/problem "![](images/07/07463b409d54d1970ee9d47cad5de7188ea44e682a58e7e941b3cd4bf7f5efa6.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-1,1)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $A$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside $M.$\n\n![](images/73/734ff08a25e25486dc65f919ce99bc5422ca8ce264bb7d044479b1eb3b6a716b.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/28/286f8b397b3c908d29678640d691558a784e31751d09c68e6b6da855c272231c.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](images/7e/7ed7726fa1a139ded4a9226bcb01c17d0ad46ef4bd03a820f033640c68778453.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "